                    System.out.println("hi welcome");

When you think of constants in Java, you might think of final static variables — but Java gives us something smarter and more powerful: Enums. Enums let you represent a fixed set of constants with type safety, readability, and built-in functionality. Let’s dive in 👇 🧩 What Makes Enums Different from Classes? Although Enums look like classes, they behave differently in many ways: Enums cannot extend another class (they implicitly extend java.lang.Enum). You cannot create Enum objects using new. Enums can implement interfaces. Enums are implicitly final — you can’t create subclasses from them. They are used for representing a fixed set of constants instead of reusable objects like regular classes. Example: // Class class Car { ... }
// Enum enum Direction { NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST; }
